nvkvm: give each rootfs slot 3 GiB of headroom

Valve sizes both A/B rootfs partitions at 5120 MiB and dd's a 5 GiB btrfs image
into each of them (`imageroot()`), never resizing afterwards -- only /home is
freshly mkfs'd to fill its partition. The image itself already uses 3.45 GiB of
that, leaving 867 MiB on 20260707.10 and roughly 374 MiB on 20260716.1, which
grew ~300 MB. The NVIDIA userspace plus a toolchain does not fit in what is
left, which is why provisioning had to trim CUDA/OpenCL, delete 352 MB of Valve's
firmware, and still refused a real OTA 42 MB short.

Partition geometry is decided once, at install, and cannot be changed on a
machine that is already installed -- so this has to be right before release.

3 GiB is deliberately more than the ~1.4 GiB we need (untrimmed userspace,
resident toolchain, firmware left alone). The extra absorbs growth in Valve's
own image: their content growing eats their 5 GiB, but if the IMAGE ever exceeds
the partition the dd fails outright rather than merely getting tight.

The filesystem written by the dd is still 5 GiB; steamos_boot.sh --image grows
it to fill the partition, on both the initial install and every OTA.

--- a/home/deck/tools/repair_device.sh
+++ b/home/deck/tools/repair_device.sh
@@ -30,7 +30,7 @@
 #
 PART_SIZE_ESP="256"
 PART_SIZE_EFI="64"
-PART_SIZE_ROOT="5120" # This should match the size from the input disk build
+PART_SIZE_ROOT="8192" # nvkvm: 5120 upstream; +3G of headroom, see the patch header
 PART_SIZE_VAR="256"
 PART_SIZE_HOME="100" # For the stub .img file we're making this can be tiny, OS expands to fill physical disk on first
                      # boot.  We make sure to specify the inode ratio explicitly when formatting.
